{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The eyeball is roughly spherical (Figure 184), with a diameter of about $2.5$ cm.\nIts white protective outer tissue is the sclera $1$, while the transparent tissue at the front is the cornea $2$."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The source describes the cornea as the transparent front part of the sclera, but a clarification is needed: the cornea joins the sclera and is not simply sclera that has become transparent.\nBehind the cornea lies the iris $3$, whose color comes from pigment in its tissue rather than from being dyed."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The anterior chamber between the cornea and iris contains aqueous humor $4$, and the opening in the iris is the pupil $5$.\nThe pupil changes size reflexively as the incoming light changes, but the source's diameter range of about $2$ to $8$ mm is only approximate and varies with conditions and between people."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "By changing the amount of light entering the eye, the pupil acts somewhat like a camera aperture.\nBehind it is the crystalline lens $6$, a transparent structure that acts as a lens rather than a simple thin membrane."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 4,
      "text": "The structure identified as muscle $7$ in the source participates in changing the lens curvature, allowing the lens to become more convex for near viewing.\nThe space behind the lens toward the back of the eye is mainly occupied by the transparent, gel-like vitreous $8$, for which the source's description as semi-liquid is approximate."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 5,
      "text": "The retina $9$ lines the inner back of the eye and is also called the net-like membrane in the source.\nThe optic nerve $10$ carries processed signals from the retina toward the brain."
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 6,
      "text": "A clarification is important: the retina contains photoreceptors and other nerve cells, and rods and cones are its photoreceptor cells, not rod-shaped or cone-shaped branches of the optic nerve.\nThese numbers retain the source's anatomical correspondences without treating its historical diagram as a complete modern anatomical drawing."
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Most of the refraction of light entering the eye occurs at the corneal surface, while the crystalline lens adds refraction and contributes to focusing.\nFor rays close to the optical axis, a paraxial approximation can represent the cornea, lens and other media together as an equivalent converging thin lens with a variable focal length."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "This model treats the retina's position as fixed, so the image distance from the equivalent lens to the image is approximately constant.\nIt is a simplification for studying image formation, not a claim that the real eye, with its several refracting surfaces, is a single thin lens."
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The retina acts as the screen in this analogy, receiving an inverted real image of the object.\nA living retina is not a flat screen, however, and light acting on rods and cones produces signals that undergo neural processing and travel toward the brain to support vision."
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "For an ideal eye without refractive error, accommodation can be relaxed when viewing a distant object while its image still falls on the retina.\nThe ciliary muscle is a ring of muscle around the lens, and the suspensory fibers are fine strands attached to the lens."
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "For near viewing, this muscle contracts and the fibers pull less on the lens.\nThe lens becomes rounder, shortening the eye's equivalent focal length so that the image falls on the retina again."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The source calls this lens compression, but the intended focusing change should be understood as a muscle-mediated change in curvature, not compression of the lens as a whole body.\nThis adjustment of focusing ability to the distance of the object is called accommodation."
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "For two object points with a fixed separation, moving the object closer increases the angle they subtend at the eye, the visual angle $\\varphi$ (Figure 185).\nA larger visual angle helps separate detail, and the source uses an angle greater than $1$ arcminute as a rough reference for distinguishing two points."
    },
    {
      "index": 14,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 4,
      "text": "Actual resolution also depends on illumination, contrast, and retinal and neural processing, so images falling on different photoreceptors alone do not guarantee that two separate points will be seen.\nMoving the object ever closer therefore does not always make it clearer, because accommodation has a limit.\nThe nearest object point that can still be brought into clear focus under given conditions is the near point of accommodation, and a closer object cannot be made clear simply by accommodating further, even though its visual angle is larger."
    },
    {
      "index": 15,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "The source calls a distance suitable for seeing detail without excessive accommodation the best viewing distance, or the distance of distinct vision.\nElementary optics commonly takes $25$ cm as a conventional reference for distinct vision, not as everyone's near point or most comfortable viewing distance."
    },
    {
      "index": 16,
      "blockIndex": 7,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Even an eye without refractive error may need stronger accommodation to view a very close object.\nThe conventional reference, an individual's nearest clear viewing distance, and comfort under current conditions must therefore be distinguished."
    },
    {
      "index": 17,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "In the ideal model without refractive error, the most distant object point seen clearly with accommodation relaxed is the far point.\nThe far point is taken to be at infinity, meaning that approximately parallel rays from a distant object can focus on the retina without accommodation."
    },
    {
      "index": 18,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Muscles that rotate the eyeballs turn their axes toward the object being viewed.\nFor a nearer object, the eyes generally turn farther inward, with corresponding changes in the activity of these muscles."
    },
    {
      "index": 19,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "The source describes a difference in muscular tension between the eyes as a distance cue, but this should not be read as a universal increase in left-right muscle differences for every direction of viewing.\nBecause the eyes view from different positions, a nearby object also produces slightly different retinal images in the two eyes."
    },
    {
      "index": 20,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The brain uses these binocular image differences, together with cues from coordinated eye movements, to help judge distances to the object and its parts.\nThese cues support three-dimensional perception rather than making muscle tension a direct distance ruler."
    },
    {
      "index": 21,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "In the optical model of myopia, light from a distant object converges in front of the retina when accommodation is relaxed (Figure 186(a)), so the retinal image of the distant object is blurred.\nThis refractive state is called myopia, or nearsightedness."
    },
    {
      "index": 22,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "An uncorrected myopic eye has a far point at a finite distance rather than at infinity, and whether a nearer object is clear also depends on its being within the available accommodation range.\nThe source's statement that the distance of distinct vision is smaller describes nearer viewing, not a single comfortable distance shared by all myopic eyes."
    },
    {
      "index": 23,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "A diverging lens changes the directions of incoming rays in this model and explains the optical principle of myopia-correcting spectacles.\nApproximately parallel rays from a distant object diverge after the lens, and their backward extensions appear to originate at the eye's far point, shown in Figure 186(b) as point $A$."
    },
    {
      "index": 24,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "If the lens makes these rays match the eye's focusing ability with accommodation relaxed, the distant image can focus on the retina.\nThis explains clear distant viewing without an additional demand for accommodation, not a guarantee that any lens gives every myopic eye the same visual state."
    },
    {
      "index": 25,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "In the optical model of hyperopia, with accommodation relaxed, light from a distant object would converge behind the retina if it continued onward (Figure 187(a)).\nThe light actually reaching the retina is then not properly focused, and this refractive state is called hyperopia, or farsightedness."
    },
    {
      "index": 26,
      "blockIndex": 13,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Some hyperopic eyes can use accommodation to compensate during distant viewing, but whether compensation is possible and how much is needed depend on the refractive state and available accommodation.\nNear viewing adds a further accommodation demand, and the source uses a distance of distinct vision greater than $25$ cm to illustrate possible difficulty with close viewing, not a fixed distance that every hyperopic eye must have."
    },
    {
      "index": 27,
      "blockIndex": 14,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "A converging lens changes incoming ray directions and explains the optical principle of hyperopia-correcting spectacles.\nFirst specify what the eye's focusing muscles are doing, for example whether they are relaxed or adjusting focus."
    },
    {
      "index": 28,
      "blockIndex": 14,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "In the distant-viewing model, the spectacle lens and the eye together must focus approximately parallel incoming rays on the retina (Figure 187(b)).\nThis happens only when the lens and the eye's focusing ability match in that chosen state."
    },
    {
      "index": 29,
      "blockIndex": 14,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "In the source's near-viewing construction, light from an object $25$ cm from the eye (Figure 187(c)) becomes less divergent after passing through the converging lens.\nBackward extensions of the rays reaching the eye appear to come from a virtual image at a distance $d>25$ cm, which the eye treats as a more distant object when focusing."
    },
    {
      "index": 30,
      "blockIndex": 14,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "Under the conditions of this construction, a suitable converging lens lets the eye see clearly at the conventional distance of distinct vision without adjusting its focus as strongly as it would without the spectacles.\nThe source's conclusion that this distance becomes the same as for a normal eye describes that simplified example, not identical comfort for everyone or advice for choosing spectacle lenses."
    }
  ],
